.pdxdoc  libpdx-elevate  1.1.2
.audience  tool-author, security-auditor, kernel-integrator
.format-version  1
.rendered-by  doc (§5.3 of design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md)

.section  NAME

libpdx-elevate — client-side elevate-protocol helper for per-op
capability requests on PaideiaOS.

.section  SYNOPSIS

  ;; assembly-level (from src/*.pdx)

  ;; --- Broker binding + wire ---
  call  elevate_client_lookup_broker
  call  elevate_client_set_target_fp_lo
  call  elevate_client_set_human_timeout
  call  elevate_client_set_fast_timeout

  ;; --- Non-dispatching probe (renamed v1.1.0) ---
  call  elevate_client_request_norealize   ;; formerly elevate_client_request

  ;; --- Full-flow requests ---
  call  elevate_client_request_ex          ;; base flow (M2)
  call  elevate_client_request_ex_j        ;; audit-first wrapper  (M3-001)
  call  elevate_client_request_ex_r        ;; retry + audit + policy (M3-002)
  call  elevate_client_request_ex_ctx      ;; explicit-context twin  (ENH-006)

  ;; --- Handle acquire / require (credential shape, ENH-001..004) ---
  call  elevate_client_acquire             ;; request_ex_r + mint + shadow-bind
  call  elevate_client_require             ;; per-op re-assert (freshness+caps)
  call  elevate_client_require_scoped      ;; require + scope + budget consume
  call  elevate_client_require_j           ;; require + per-op audit record

  ;; --- Cap-lifetime enforcement (M2-003, LE.M3) ---
  call  elevate_client_cap_mint
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_expire
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_expire_abs
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_grant
  call  elevate_client_cap_get_grant
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_scope
  call  elevate_client_cap_get_scope
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_budget
  call  elevate_client_cap_get_budget_remaining
  call  elevate_client_cap_consume_budget
  call  elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke
  call  elevate_client_cap_derive          ;; LE.M3-001 (v1.1.1 mint_ctx_ptr)
  call  elevate_client_cap_bind_parent     ;; LE.M3-002
  call  elevate_client_cap_get_parent      ;; LE.M3-002
  call  elevate_client_cap_get_depth       ;; LE.M3-002 (bounded walk)

  ;; --- Audit records (ENH-004) ---
  call  elevate_client_journal_op

  ;; --- Outcome reduction (R90-XREPO.011.M1-006) ---
  call  elevate_client_classify_outcome
  call  elevate_client_request_outcome

  ;; conceptual (tool-side)
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read,  "svc.elevate-broker")>
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, broker_ep)>
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(mint,  reply)>
  request  caps=<mask>, dur=<ns>, target_fp_lo=<user>
  receive  Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(0x191, expire_ns)>

.section  DESCRIPTION

libpdx-elevate wraps the R48.M7 kernel elevate protocol
(`src/kernel/core/ipc/elevate_channel.pdx`) with the client-side state
machine tools need to request a bounded-lifetime capability from
`svc.elevate-broker`.  It is a shared library — no binary, no daemon.
Consumers are `pkg` (§5.1, elevate for `/pkgs` write), the R50
coreutils (`cp` and `mv` for cross-subtree destinations), the R50
filesystem tools (`mount.pdxfs` / `umount.pdxfs` / `mkfs.pdxfs`, all
fail-closed today pending broker-cap plumbing), and every future tool
that needs to escalate a single operation without becoming setuid or
claiming persistent supervisor authority.

The library does not replace `sudo`.  There is no `sudo` in PaideiaOS
(see .see-also §5 of design/user/model.md).  What replaces it is the
per-op elevate protocol: for one operation, at one moment, with one
duration budget and one cap-mask scope, the caller asks the founder (or
the auto-approve policy table) for a `Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>`.  The
cap dies at its expire_ns.  No lingering root shell exists to hijack;
no persistent group membership grants standing authority; every request
is journaled to `/system/audit/user-events/`.

Since v1.1.0 the recommended surface for consumers holding a handle
across more than a single wire hop is the acquire/require pair rather
than a bare request:

  * `elevate_client_acquire` performs one full REQ+APR round-trip
    (retry+audit+policy) and returns a `row_id` HANDLE (< 16),
    shadow-binding the granted caps and the broker's absolute
    `expire_ns` in one call.
  * `elevate_client_require` (and its `_scoped` / `_j` variants) is
    the per-op re-assert against that handle: freshness plus caps
    subset, with NO broker round-trip and NO shell-timestamp
    persistence.  Grant amplification (1 REQ/APR pair, N mutating
    uses) is legible to an auditor via the per-op record
    `elevate_client_journal_op` writes.

.section  INVARIANTS  I4  I5  I7  D3  D4

Every entry point in libpdx-elevate enforces the following:

  D3 (audit-first)
    Every mutating flow (request_ex_j, request_ex_r, require_j) writes
    its REQ or per-op audit record before it reaches the broker or
    marks the op as authorized.  A REQ-journal failure aborts the
    operation with ELVJ_ERR_REQ_JOURNAL_FAIL — no wire hop occurs.
    Successful APR writes its APR audit record before the cap is
    returned to the caller.  ELVJ_ERR_OP_JOURNAL_FAIL is the require_j
    counterpart: the op was authorized but the audit record recording
    that fact did not land; the caller should NOT treat the op as
    audit-covered.

  D4 (dual-signed manifest)
    libpdx-elevate ships with manifest.pdxsig carrying author +
    Paideia-root ML-DSA-65 signatures.  `pkg install libpdx-elevate`
    verifies both before the .pdx sources are placed under /pkgs/.

  I4 (never silent failure)
    Every entry point returns a nonzero status code on the failure
    path (see .section STATUS CODES).  No path silently returns
    success on partial completion.  The client-side outcome reducer
    `elevate_client_classify_outcome` folds every ELV*_ERR_* value
    into a three-valued (ALLOW / DENY / TIMEOUT) enum with the
    org-wide fail-closed default (any unrecognized value -> DENY).

  I5 (destructive ops undoable)
    Not applicable at library level — libpdx-elevate holds no state
    on the caller's behalf.  The Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL> lifetime
    bound (M2-003) is the equivalent invariant: the cap self-
    invalidates at expire_ns and calls to it thereafter fail with
    ELCC_STATE_REVOKED.  A caller cannot "forget" to release it.
    LE.M3 adds a depth-bounded delegation chain
    (ELCC_MAX_DELEGATION_DEPTH = 4) so a child cap can never outlive
    its parent or exceed its rights.

  I7 (help + tutorial + example-gallery)
    This .pdxdoc file is the doc(1) back-end.  Callers hit --help
    against a consuming tool (e.g. `pkg --help`), which references
    this doc via .see-also.  --tutorial and --examples for
    libpdx-elevate specifically will land with the doc tool itself
    (§5.3, doc.M2); today the .examples section below stands in.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_lookup_broker

.signature  fn elevate_client_lookup_broker() -> ep_id | ELVC_ERR_LOOKUP_FAIL

Resolves the canonical name `svc.elevate-broker` to a
KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT handle via `svc_lookup`.  No side effects; safe to
call at any point in the caller's lifecycle.  The returned handle is
suitable as the `broker_ep_id` argument to `elevate_client_send_req`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_set_human_timeout / _set_fast_timeout

.signature  fn elevate_client_set_human_timeout(ns: u64)
.signature  fn elevate_client_set_fast_timeout(ns: u64)

Set the recv-timeout budget for the human-approve and auto-approve
paths respectively.  Defaults are 30 s and 500 ms.  Callers that
raise the human budget above 30 s should also raise their own
user-visible progress indication — the founder may take that long to
respond, and the terminal will otherwise appear stuck.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_norealize

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_norealize(caps: u64, dur: u64,
.signature                                      req_buf: *u8, reply_buf: *u8)
.signature                                      -> ELVC_NOT_DISPATCHED | ELVC_ERR_*

Renamed from `elevate_client_request` at ENH-005 (#12).  The M1
send-and-block skeleton preserved from v1.0.0 for callers who
legitimately want the non-dispatching probe (frame-assemble +
broker-name lookup, no wire hop).  Its ONLY non-error return is
`ELVC_NOT_DISPATCHED` (0xFFFFEA00, formerly `ELVC_STUB`); consumers
MUST NOT read that code as authorization.  Callers wanting a real
dispatch use `elevate_client_request_ex*` or `elevate_client_acquire`.
See the file header WHY THE RENAME note in `src/elevate_client.pdx`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex(caps: u64, dur: u64, req_buf: *u8,
.signature                               reply_ep_id: u32, reply_buf: *u8,
.signature                               timeout_ns: u64) -> ELVC_*

The full M2 flow.  Sequence: policy consult (fast/human classification)
→ REQ assemble via `elevate_request_write_frame` → REQ send via
`elevate_client_send_req` → APR recv via `elevate_client_recv_reply` →
grant subset check via `elevate_client_check_grant`.
`timeout_ns == 0` picks the human or fast default based on the policy
classification.  `target_fp_lo` comes from the process-global set via
`elevate_client_set_target_fp_lo`.  Caller must supply a
64-byte-aligned `req_buf` and `reply_buf`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex_ctx

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex_ctx(caps: u64, dur: u64,
.signature                                   req_buf: *u8, reply_ep_id: u32,
.signature                                   reply_buf: *u8, ctx_buf: *u8)
.signature                                   -> ELVC_*/ELVC_ERR_BAD_CTX

ENH-006 (#17) explicit-context twin of `elevate_client_request_ex`.
Reads `target_fp_lo`, fast timeout, human timeout, and an explicit
per-call timeout from a caller-owned `ctx_buf` (see
`ELVC_CTX_*_OFF` layout constants) instead of the three process-
global mutable slots, so concurrent flows in one process do not race
each other.  Refuses `ELVC_ERR_BAD_CTX` (0xFFFFEA06) on a null
ctx_buf.  Scoped to the core primitive only; full singleton
retirement of `_ex_j` / `_ex_r` / `elevate_client_acquire` is deferred
until a concurrent consumer surfaces.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex_j

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex_j(caps: u64, dur: u64, ...)
.signature                                 -> ELVC_*/ELVJ_ERR_*

M3-001 audit-first wrapper.  Journals REQ before wire hop; aborts on
REQ-journal failure; journals APR on success; returns
ELVJ_ERR_APR_JOURNAL_FAIL if the APR-journal write fails after grant
(caller should revoke immediately in that case — the audit chain is
broken otherwise).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex_r

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex_r(caps: u64, dur: u64, ...)
.signature                                 -> ELVC_*/ELVJ_ERR_*/ELVR_ERR_*

M3-002 retry wrapper over _ex_j.  Retriable set is
{ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT, ELVC_ERR_SEND_FAIL, ELVC_ERR_LOOKUP_FAIL}.  Bounds
[1, 8] attempts (default 3).  Backoff 100 ms → 400 ms → 1.6 s
(capped).  Every attempt writes its own REQ audit record; the eventual
success writes its APR record; the auditor sees a per-attempt trail.
Non-retriable failures pass through unchanged on the first attempt.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_acquire

.signature  fn elevate_client_acquire(caps: u64, dur: u64, req_buf: *u8,
.signature                            reply_ep_id: u64, reply_buf: *u8,
.signature                            mint_ctx_buf: *u8) -> row_id
.signature                            | ELCA_ERR_BAD_BUF | ELVC_*/ELVR_*/ELCC_*

ENH-001 (#11) composed handle acquire.  Runs `request_ex_r`
(retry+journal+dispatch), then on success mints a
Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL> via `elevate_client_cap_mint` and shadow-
binds the broker's absolute `expire_ns` (`bind_expire_abs`) plus the
granted caps (`bind_grant`) in one call.  Returns a `row_id` HANDLE
(< 16) discriminated from every error band (>= 0xFFFFEA00) by a bare
`cmp rax, 16; jae refuse`.  See `src/elevate_client_acquire.pdx`'s
mint_ctx_buf LAYOUT (40 bytes, 5 u64 words: parent_ep_slot /
request_id / requester_pid / target_cap_kind / target_cap_rights) for
the caller-owned scratch buffer format.  ELCA_ERR_BAD_BUF
(0xFFFFEA50) is the ONLY code this function owns; every other refusal
is a passthrough from a composed call.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_require / _require_scoped / _require_j

.signature  fn elevate_client_require(row_id: u64, needed_caps: u64)
.signature                            -> ELCA_OK | ELCA_ERR_*/ELCC_ERR_*
.signature  fn elevate_client_require_scoped(row_id: u64, needed_caps: u64,
.signature                                   scope_fp: u64)
.signature                                   -> ELCA_OK | ELCA_ERR_*/ELCC_ERR_*
.signature  fn elevate_client_require_j(actor_fp_lo: u64, row_id: u64,
.signature                              needed_caps: u64)
.signature                              -> ELCA_OK | ELCA_ERR_*/ELVJ_ERR_*

ENH-002 (#13), ENH-003 (#15), ENH-004 (#16) per-op re-assert against a
handle from `elevate_client_acquire`.  No broker hop — freshness
(`elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke`) + caps subset against the
shadow grant map (`elevate_client_cap_get_grant`).

  * `_scoped` adds an optional per-handle scope fingerprint match and
    a per-handle bounded op count (`bind_scope` / `bind_budget`); a
    handle that never binds them behaves identically to plain
    `_require`.
  * `_j` adds one `elevate_client_journal_op` per authorized use so
    grant amplification is legible to an auditor.  Refused checks are
    NOT journaled here (see `elevate_client_journal_op`'s doc for
    why); the require stats table records attempted checks
    regardless.

Every ELCC_ERR_* refusal (BAD_ROW, NO_DEADLINE, NO_GRANT) passes
through unchanged so the caller can distinguish caller-side usage
errors from per-op refusals.  ELCA_ERR_EXPIRED (0xFFFFEA60) and
ELCA_ERR_CAPS_INSUFFICIENT (0xFFFFEA61) are the per-op refusals;
`_scoped` adds ELCA_ERR_SCOPE_MISMATCH (0xFFFFEA62).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_mint / _bind_expire / _bind_expire_abs / _check_and_revoke

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_mint(...) -> Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_expire(row_id: u32, dur_ns: u64)
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_expire_abs(row_id: u32, deadline_ns: u64)
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_expired(row_id: u32) -> u32
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke(row_id: u32) -> ELCC_*

M2-003 cap-lifetime wrapper.  Kernel default `expire_ns == 0` is
compensated by a client-side shadow deadline map (16 slots, one per
possible row_id).  `bind_expire` computes `hpet_now_ns() + dur_ns`;
`bind_expire_abs` stores an absolute value the broker's APR already
carried without re-adding `hpet_now_ns` (ENH-001).
`check_and_revoke` invokes kernel `elevate_channel_cap_revoke` when
past deadline; the call is idempotent (`REVOKE_ALREADY` collapses to
`STATE_REVOKED`).

Callers should invoke `check_and_revoke` at every re-entry to a
guarded region; the M4 witness `elevate_client_cap_witness`
demonstrates the pattern (12 stages, fingerprint
`LIBPDX-ELEVATE M4 CAP OK`).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_bind_grant / _get_grant

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_grant(row_id: u64, granted_caps: u64)
.signature                                    -> ELCC_OK | ELCC_ERR_BAD_ROW
.signature                                    | ELCC_ERR_ZERO_GRANT
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_get_grant(row_id: u64) -> granted_caps | 0

ENH-001 (#11) shadow the APR's `granted_caps` bitmask per row.
`elevate_client_acquire` calls `bind_grant` immediately after a
successful mint using the SAME granted_caps it already validated as a
subset of the request.  `bind_grant` refuses `granted_caps == 0` with
`ELCC_ERR_ZERO_GRANT` (0xFFFFEA73, extension band, v1.1.2 LE.M1-polish
G13) — distinct from `ELCC_ERR_BAD_ROW` so a caller can tell "row_id
out of range" from "grant mask empty".  `get_grant` returns 0 for a
row that never bound (out of range OR minted without going through
`elevate_client_acquire`); `elevate_client_require` treats that as
`ELCC_ERR_NO_GRANT` (0xFFFFEA38).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_bind_scope / _get_scope

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_scope(row_id: u64, scope_fp: u64)
.signature                                    -> ELCC_OK | ELCC_ERR_BAD_ROW
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_get_scope(row_id: u64) -> scope_fp | 0

ENH-003 (#15) shadow an opaque scope fingerprint per row.  `0` clears
(unbound = matches ANY `scope_fp` a caller later presents to
`require_scoped`).  The value is opaque to this library — the caller
picks the fingerprint (typically a hash of a recursive walk's root
path); no filesystem vocabulary is imposed.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_bind_budget / _get_budget_remaining / _consume_budget

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_budget(row_id: u64, max_ops: u64)
.signature                                     -> ELCC_OK | ELCC_ERR_BAD_ROW
.signature                                     | ELCC_ERR_BAD_BUDGET
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_get_budget_remaining(row_id: u64) -> u64
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_consume_budget(row_id: u64)
.signature                                        -> ELCC_OK | ELCC_ERR_BAD_ROW
.signature                                        | ELCC_ERR_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED

ENH-003 (#15) per-handle bounded op count.  Storage encoding is
`remaining + 1` so a bound handle at exhaustion returns a distinct
`ELCC_ERR_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED` (0xFFFFEA3A) forever, rather than
wrapping to "unbounded" the next call.  `bind_budget` refuses
`max_ops > ELCC_MAX_BUDGET` with `ELCC_ERR_BAD_BUDGET` (0xFFFFEA39).
`consume_budget` decrements the counter; on an unbudgeted handle
(never bound) it silently returns OK.  `require_scoped` composes the
budget consume with the freshness + caps check.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_derive / _bind_parent / _get_parent / _get_depth

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_derive(parent_row: u64, child_caps: u64,
.signature                               child_dur_ns: u64,
.signature                               mint_ctx_ptr: *u8)
.signature                               -> child_row_id | ELCC_ERR_*
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_parent(child: u64, parent: u64)
.signature                                    -> ELCC_OK | ELCC_ERR_*
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_get_parent(row_id: u64) -> parent | 0
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_get_depth(row_id: u64) -> depth
.signature                                                  | ELCC_ERR_CYCLE_DETECTED

LE.M3-001..003 multilevel delegation.  `derive` mints a fresh
KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL row narrowed to `child_caps` (subset-checked
against the parent's granted mask), with a deadline clamped to
`min(now + child_dur_ns, parent_expire)` so a child can never outlive
its parent, and records the child->parent edge via `bind_parent`.
`mint_ctx_ptr` (v1.1.1 fix) points at a caller-owned 24-byte scratch
buffer — `[+0] parent_ep_slot`, `[+8] request_id`,
`[+16] requester_pid` — layout-compatible with the FIRST three words
of `elevate_client_acquire`'s `mint_ctx_buf`.  A null `mint_ctx_ptr`
is refused before any nested call with `ELCC_ERR_BAD_CTX`
(0xFFFFEA72, extension band).

`get_depth` is a bounded walk of the parent map returning
`ELCC_ERR_CYCLE_DETECTED` (0xFFFFEA71) if the map is corrupted past
`ELCC_MAX_DELEGATION_DEPTH = 4` hops; `bind_parent`'s SELF_LOOP and
ALREADY_BOUND gates make the happy-path unreachable, so the cycle
sentinel is a corruption-defense signal.  `derive` refuses
`ELCC_ERR_DELEGATION_TOO_DEEP` (0xFFFFEA3F) when
`get_depth(parent) >= ELCC_MAX_DELEGATION_DEPTH - 1`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_journal_op

.signature  fn elevate_client_journal_op(actor_fp_lo: u64, row_id: u64,
.signature                               needed_caps: u64)
.signature                               -> ELVJ_OK | ELVJ_ERR_OP_JOURNAL_FAIL

ENH-004 (#16) per-op audit record for a use of an acquired handle
(`ELVJ_EVT_OP = 3`).  `elevate_client_require_j` calls this on
`ELCA_OK` (an authorized use); a refused check is NOT journaled here
because journaling refusals would make the per-op write count
meaningless as a grant-amplification signal.
`ELVJ_ERR_OP_JOURNAL_FAIL` (0xFFFFEA43) means the op was authorized
but the audit record recording that fact did not land; the caller
should NOT treat the op as audit-covered (retry the journal write out
of band, or abort if audit coverage is a hard requirement).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_classify_outcome / _request_outcome

.signature  fn elevate_client_classify_outcome(status: u64) -> ELVC_OUTCOME_*
.signature  fn elevate_client_request_outcome(caps: u64, dur: u64, ...)
.signature                                     -> ELVC_OUTCOME_*

R90-XREPO.011.M1-006 (#18) client-side ALLOW / DENY / TIMEOUT
reconciliation.  Reduces the open-ended `ELV*_ERR_*` taxonomy the
transport / retry layers speak in to three enum ordinals:

  * `ELVC_OUTCOME_ALLOW   = 0`  (matches `ELVC_OK`)
  * `ELVC_OUTCOME_DENY    = 1`  (broker DENY / packer / journal / policy
                                  failures, unknown values -- fail-closed)
  * `ELVC_OUTCOME_TIMEOUT = 2`  (`ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT` /
                                  `ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED`)

None of the three collide with any status-band value (all
`ELV*_ERR_*` are >= `0xFFFFEA00`), so a caller passing an
`ELVC_OUTCOME_*` back into a status-shaped entry point trips a
bounded-value check down-chain rather than silently proceeding.
Applies the org-wide fail-closed policy (paideia-os#2121) at the
client boundary.

.section  STATUS  CODES

Codes are grouped by band; the numeric value is a u32 return code.
Every entry point returns 0 on success.

  0xFFFFEA00  ELVC_NOT_DISPATCHED        non-dispatching probe's only
                                         non-error return (renamed from
                                         ELVC_STUB, ENH-005 #12); MUST NOT
                                         be read as authorization
  0xFFFFEA01  ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT           recv-timeout expired (retriable)
  0xFFFFEA02  ELVC_ERR_BAD_REPLY         reply frame malformed (non-retriable)
  0xFFFFEA03  ELVC_ERR_GRANT_INVALID     granted caps not subset of requested
  0xFFFFEA04  ELVC_ERR_BAD_EXPIRE        expire_ns == 0 in APR
  0xFFFFEA05  ELVC_ERR_BAD_REPLY_EP      caller-supplied reply_ep_id invalid
  0xFFFFEA06  ELVC_ERR_BAD_CTX           ENH-006 (#17): ctx_buf is null in
                                         elevate_client_request_ex_ctx
  0xFFFFEA0B  ELVC_ERR_BAD_BUF           req_buf or reply_buf is null
  0xFFFFEA0E  ELVC_ERR_PACK_FAIL         REQ frame assemble refused
  0xFFFFEA0F  ELVC_ERR_LOOKUP_FAIL       svc_lookup returned SVC_LOOKUP_NONE
  0xFFFFEA10..1F  ELCP_ERR_*             policy table (BAD_ARG, RATE_LIMIT,
                                         NO_MATCH, ENOSPC, BAD_DUR, BAD_CAPS)
  0xFFFFEA20  ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED         retry attempts exhausted
  0xFFFFEA21  ELVR_ERR_BAD_ARG           retry config out of bounds
  0xFFFFEA30..3A  ELCC_*                 cap-lifetime base band
                                         (BAD_ROW, MINT_FAIL, NO_DEADLINE,
                                         REVOKE_FAIL, STATE_LIVE, EXPIRED,
                                         REVOKED, NOTE_NO_NARROW, NO_GRANT,
                                         BAD_BUDGET, BUDGET_EXHAUSTED)
  0xFFFFEA3B  ELCC_ERR_CHILD_CAPS_ESCALATION  LE.M3-001 (#26): derive with
                                         child mask exceeding parent grant
  0xFFFFEA3C  ELCC_ERR_PARENT_EXPIRED    LE.M3-001 (#26): parent's shadow
                                         deadline past (or 0 = unbound)
  0xFFFFEA3D  ELCC_ERR_PARENT_SELF_LOOP  LE.M3-002 (#27): bind_parent(r,r)
  0xFFFFEA3E  ELCC_ERR_PARENT_ALREADY_BOUND  LE.M3-002 (#27): rebind attempt
  0xFFFFEA3F  ELCC_ERR_DELEGATION_TOO_DEEP  LE.M3-003 (#28): depth >= MAX-1
  0xFFFFEA40  ELVJ_ERR_BAD_ACTOR         no target_fp_lo set (process-global)
  0xFFFFEA41  ELVJ_ERR_REQ_JOURNAL_FAIL  audit REQ write failed; abort
  0xFFFFEA42  ELVJ_ERR_APR_JOURNAL_FAIL  audit APR write failed; revoke grant
  0xFFFFEA43  ELVJ_ERR_OP_JOURNAL_FAIL   ENH-004 (#16): per-op audit record
                                         write failed (op WAS authorized)
  0xFFFFEA50  ELCA_ERR_BAD_BUF           ENH-001 (#11): acquire's mint_ctx_buf
                                         is null
  0xFFFFEA51  ELCA_ERR_BAD_REPLY_BUF     ENH-001 (#11): reply_buf is null
  0xFFFFEA60  ELCA_ERR_EXPIRED           ENH-002 (#13): handle deadline passed
  0xFFFFEA61  ELCA_ERR_CAPS_INSUFFICIENT ENH-002 (#13): needed_caps not a
                                         subset of granted_caps
  0xFFFFEA62  ELCA_ERR_SCOPE_MISMATCH    ENH-003 (#15): require_scoped scope_fp
                                         did not match bound scope
  0xFFFFEA71  ELCC_ERR_CYCLE_DETECTED    LE.M3-002 (#27, extension band):
                                         get_depth walker exhausted its bound
  0xFFFFEA72  ELCC_ERR_BAD_CTX           LE.M3-001 (v1.1.1 #26, extension
                                         band): derive with null mint_ctx_ptr
  0xFFFFEA73  ELCC_ERR_ZERO_GRANT        LE.M1-polish (v1.1.2 #47, extension
                                         band): bind_grant with
                                         granted_caps == 0

Outcome enum (`ElevateClientOutcome`, separate namespace):

  0           ELVC_OUTCOME_ALLOW         only ELVC_OK reduces here
  1           ELVC_OUTCOME_DENY          fail-closed default; broker DENY,
                                         packer / journal / policy failures,
                                         unknown values
  2           ELVC_OUTCOME_TIMEOUT       ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT or
                                         ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED

Consumers map these to the shared exit codes (design/tooling/plan.md
§4.1 I4 table): every ELVC_/ELCP_/ELCC_/ELCA_ failure is exit 4
(capability denied); ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED is exit 3 (system error);
ELVJ_ERR_* is exit 4 (capability + audit denied).  `_classify_outcome`
folds this taxonomy for callers whose disposition only needs the
three-way distinction.

.section  EXAMPLES

  ;; #1 — happy-path per-op elevate, auto-approve
  ;;
  ;; A tool wants to write /pkgs for a package install.  target_fp_lo
  ;; is the founder's user fingerprint.  Caller pre-mints a reply
  ;; endpoint and a request buffer.
  ;;
  ;;   caps       = 0x0000000000000002  ;; KIND_PDXFS_FILE(write, /pkgs)
  ;;   dur        = 60_000_000_000       ;; 60 s
  ;;   timeout_ns = 0                    ;; use policy default
  ;;
  ;;   elevate_client_set_target_fp_lo   founder_fp_lo
  ;;   elevate_client_request_ex_r       caps, dur, req_buf, reply_ep,
  ;;                                     reply_buf, timeout_ns
  ;;   ;; on success rax=0, reply_buf holds APR frame

  ;; #2 — cap-lifetime enforcement at re-entry
  ;;
  ;;   loop:
  ;;     elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke   row_id
  ;;     cmp     rax, ELCC_STATE_LIVE
  ;;     jne     .cap_expired_or_bad
  ;;     ;; ...perform guarded work using the cap...
  ;;     jmp     loop
  ;;   .cap_expired_or_bad:
  ;;     ;; cap dead — either re-request or bail

  ;; #3 — credential-shape acquire + require (ENH-001..004)
  ;;
  ;;   elevate_client_acquire   caps, dur, req_buf, reply_ep, reply_buf,
  ;;                            mint_ctx_buf
  ;;   cmp     rax, 16
  ;;   jae     .refused                     ;; every error is >= 0xFFFFEA00
  ;;   mov     [handle], rax                ;; row_id (0..15)
  ;;   ;; ...one broker round-trip; now N per-op re-asserts:
  ;;   .op_start:
  ;;     mov     rdi, [handle]
  ;;     mov     rsi, needed_caps
  ;;     call    elevate_client_require     ;; no broker hop
  ;;     cmp     rax, 0                     ;; ELCA_OK
  ;;     jne     .refused
  ;;     ;; ...do the guarded op...

  ;; #4 — outcome reduction for a caller that only branches on ALLOW/DENY/TIMEOUT
  ;;
  ;;   call    elevate_client_request_outcome
  ;;   cmp     rax, 0                       ;; ELVC_OUTCOME_ALLOW
  ;;   je      .allowed
  ;;   cmp     rax, 2                       ;; ELVC_OUTCOME_TIMEOUT
  ;;   je      .timed_out
  ;;   ;; fall through: DENY (fail-closed default)

.section  POSIX  DIFFERENCES

The Unix analog is `sudo`.  libpdx-elevate is different on five
observable axes:

  1. sudo grants a shell.  libpdx-elevate grants a cap for one op.
  2. sudo persists for a tty timeout (default 5 min).  libpdx-elevate
     caps carry an explicit expire_ns; the kernel invalidates at
     deadline; no session-time inheritance.
  3. sudo authenticates once and remembers.  libpdx-elevate consults
     the policy table for auto-approve, or blocks on the founder for
     human approve, per invocation.  There is no "sudo timestamp".
  4. sudo works via setuid + /etc/sudoers.  libpdx-elevate mints a
     kernel-checked `Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>` — the kernel enforces
     the scope; no in-kernel superuser exists to bypass.  See §3.3
     of design/user/model.md ("Founder is not magic").
  5. sudo logs to /var/log/auth.log optionally.  libpdx-elevate
     journals every REQ, APR, and per-op use to
     /system/audit/user-events/ before the wire hop (D3 audit-first).

